      "Language": "eng",
      "Abstract": {"AbstractText": "A 6-year-old boy presented with a large, rapidly growing osteosarcoma of the upper humerus and severe neuropathic arm pain. Despite large doses of morphine (100 micrograms/kg/hr), which resulted in intermittent somnolence and respiratory depression, his pain was poorly controlled. An interscalene brachial plexus catheter was inserted, and bupivacaine was injected on ten occasions over 5 days, with markedly improved analgesia and decreased opioid requirement. Cancer pain in children can be controlled by opioids in 95% of cases; however, circumstances such as intractable neuropathic pain may require specific regional anesthetic techniques."},
